The thing is - moving images as such is really simple. I mean, after all, we're 
just talking about 400K of data, if we're talking about the contents. Any Unix 
system can run dd, to read or write the data from whereever.
The whole problem, and why teledisk is neccesary, is that it's a hardware 
problem, not a software problem.
The RX50 uses a format that you *never* find on any other kind of hardware.
So it's the simulation of that hardware that is the whole obstacle, and teledisk 
was/is pretty good at going around that obstacle.
There is very few other pieces of software that will do this for you.
I remember that there also were some kind of RX50 device driver for DOS around, 
which did something similar to teledisk with the hardware. But I have never seen 
much of anything else that can do it.

I think you're too much focused on the contents of the floppies, as if that were 
the problem when it's not. The problem is the hardware format of the floppy, 
something other floppy systems can't normally cope with.
If you get the data contents of the RX50 down on your computer, you can access 
it from E11, SimH and so forth. No problem. And getting the software contents 
isn't that difficult. I'm sure several people can help with it, if it hasn't 
already been done (I think I saw someone mention a tool to extract the data from 
the teledisk image, even).
However, having the data, you won't be able to recreate an RX50 again, unless 
you actually have a machine with an RX50 drive (or use teledisk or something 
equivalent, combined with the right hardware).

The whole point of teledisk was the ability to recreate RX50 disks using a PC.
And that requires a lot more than just writing the bytes back to a floppy on the 
PC, since a PC floppy normally have a very different hardware format than an RX50.

