It can run normal PDP-11 software that's in user mode, sure.
RT-11 have a few special device drivers to deal with the PRO as well. As long as 
you use the device drivers to communicate, you'll be fine. However, if you try 
to play with the hardware directly, you won't get far with programs from a 
"normal" PDP-11. The most obvious problem will be that you don't have the 
console DL-11, which *any* normal PDP-11 will have.

> As for the built in Keyboard/display, My Terak's all do that too.  But it
> is mapped to the address of a normal PDP-11 console so real PDP software
> runs just fine.  Plus, you can turn it off and set the serial port to
> the Console SLU address and run a terminal on it instead.  The only non-
> compatable PDP-11 device is the floppy, which is not the same in hardware
> as an RX01/RX02.  But an RT-11 driver was provided and enough info to
> write a driver for other OSes (which I plan to do at some point, just so
> I can put 8" disks on some of my other systems.)

And the PRO don't do that. As I mentioned above. The PRO don't have any DL-11 at 
the console CSR address. Nada. No simulation of a normal PDP-11 even. It's a 
PRO, you play with it the PRO way, not the PDP-11 way. :-)

Actually, all the documentation for the PRO hardware is on the net, so you can 
check it out yourself, on how the machine works.

>> (Don't ask me why DEC did it like that. It's a pain, and another reason the PRO 
